response:
 Since pentagon $ABCDE$ is regular, all its interior angles are equal. The sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is \(540^\circ\), so each interior angle measures \(540^\circ / 5 = 108^\circ\). The exterior angle at each vertex is the supplement of the interior angle, so each exterior angle of the pentagon is \(180^\circ - 108^\circ = 72^\circ\). Similarly, since hexagon \(AEFGHI\) is regular, all its interior angles are equal. The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \(720^\circ\), so each interior angle measures \(720^\circ / 6 = 120^\circ\). The exterior angle at each vertex is the supplement of the interior angle, so each exterior angle of the hexagon is \(180^\circ - 120^\circ = 60^\circ\). The exterior angle \(DEF\) is the sum of the exterior angles at vertices \(D\) and \(E\) of the pentagon and hexagon, respectively. Therefore, \(\angle DEF = 72^\circ + 60^\circ = 132^\circ\). User: $\boxed{132}$ User: If the regular polygon $ABCDEF$ is rotated about its center $O$ by $90^\circ$ clockwise, what is the degree measure of exterior angle $DEF$? [asy]
pair A,B,C,D,E,F,O;
O=(0,0);
A=dir(90);
B=dir(90+60);
C=dir(90+2*60);
D=dir(90+3*60);
E=dir(90+4*60);
F=dir(90+5*60);
draw(A--B--C--D--E--F--cycle,linewidth(1));
dot(O);
label("$O$",O,dir(0));
[/asy]
